"r2qk2r/1bppbppp/p1n2n2/1p2p3/4P3/1BNP1N2/PPP2PPP/R1BQ1RK1 b kq -":{"variation":"arkhangelsk-variation","moves":[{"uci":"e8g8","notes":[{"text":"Most Arkhangelsk games reach this position, and you have several options from here.","original":"This is really the position where most of the Arkhangelsk games reach and where most of the games branch out. As you can see, black has developed their pieces first rather than getting their king out of the center straight away. White has multiple options from this point on."}]}]},
"r2q1rk1/1bppbppp/p1n2n2/1p2p3/4P3/1BNP1N2/PPP2PPP/R1BQ1RK1 w - -":{"variation":"arkhangelsk-variation","moves":[{"uci":"c1d2","book":true,"notes":[{"text":"The most common move: it develops your bishop and prepares for Black's knight to leave c6.","original":"This is the most common move after black castles. The idea is simply to develop the bishop from its initial c1 square. It also protects the a5 square when the c-knight decides to move so that black doesn't threaten to take the light-squared bishop. You can study further by studying Judit Polgar's and Peter Svidler's games from this position. One game was drawn and the other, black won."}],"games":[9,26,65,56,41]},{"uci":"f1e1","notes":[{"text":"A sideline: your rook guards e4 so you can push the d-pawn.","original":"This is the 4th most common sideline. The idea is to push the d-pawn and to protect the pawn on e4.","ideas":[{"type":"purpose","from":"e1","relation":"defends","targets":["e4"]}]}],"games":[42,118,226,173,105]},{"uci":"c3d5","notes":[{"text":"An aggressive choice: you offer a knight trade, which also indirectly hits the c6 knight, then aim for c4.","original":"This is the 3rd most common variation and it's one of the most aggressive variations. The idea here is to exchange the knights and to indirectly attack the knight on c6 as well. Afterwards, white will aim to push for c4 and possibly exchange there. The c4 square can turn into a good outpost for one of white's pieces.","ideas":[{"type":"purpose","from":"d5","relation":"attacks","targets":["f6"]},{"type":"threat","side":"white","move":"d5f6","answer":"f6d5"}]}],"games":[83,315,634,742,635]},{"uci":"a2a3","notes":[{"text":"The second most common move: your a-pawn controls b4, so Black's knight gets no outpost there.","original":"The 2nd most common variation is the move a3. This simply prevents the knight from ever trying to set up an outpost on b4 and white will try to control that square.","ideas":[{"type":"purpose","from":"a3","relation":"controls","targets":["b4"]}]}],"games":[69,192,339,336,276]},{"uci":"a2a4","notes":[{"text":"Not very common, but simple: your a-pawn hits b5 to open the file for your rook.","original":"This is not a very common move but it holds a very simple concept. The idea is to simply try to open up the file for white's rook. Black's usual response from here is to just push the pawn. The knight could try to remaneuver itself on to the g3 square like many Closed Ruy Lopez lines or it can try to exchange itself for the dark-squared bishop.","ideas":[{"type":"purpose","from":"a4","relation":"attacks","targets":["b5"]}]}],"games":[38,125,181,182,135]}]},
"r2qk2r/1bpp1ppp/p1n2n2/1pb1p3/4P3/1B1P1N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQ1RK1 w kq -":{"variation":"arkhangelsk-variation","moves":[{"uci":"b1c3","book":true,"notes":[{"text":"You simply develop another piece with this knight move.","original":"White continues normally and develops."}]}]},
