"r1bqkb1r/ppppnppp/2n5/1B2p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq -":{"variation":"cozio-defence","moves":[{"uci":"e1g1","book":true,"notes":[{"text":"Castle first: your king's safety comes before everything else.","original":"Your king's safety first and foremost."}],"games":[181123,476769,728568,706693,466643]},{"uci":"b1c3","notes":[{"text":"Nc3 is an uncommon sideline; against ...g6, play d4 and Nd5, then trade pieces on f6 with check.","original":"4.Nc3, uncommon in the Spanish, is the main sideline, where 4...g6 is the Paulsen variation. There there is 5.d4! exd4, and White's idea is not to recapture immediately but to play 6.Nd5. 6...Bg7 7.Bg5! h6 8.Bf6 Bxf6 9.Nxf6+ Kf8.","ideas":[{"type":"line","moves":["g7g6","d2d4","e5d4","c3d5","f8g7","c1g5","h7h6","g5f6","g7f6","d5f6","e8f8"],"verdict":"whiteClearly"}]}],"games":[91455,226793,294332,202656,79518]},{"uci":"c2c3","notes":[{"text":"This was a main approach in the 19th century; Zukertort beat Anderssen in a twelve-move miniature with it.","original":"4.c3 was one of the main approaches in the 19th century, as recommended by Carl Jaenisch over 4.d4 in 1848, writing that it \"affords Black no satisfactory method of developing his game.\" In 1865, Zukertort scored a win over Anderssen in a twelve move miniature with 4.c3 d6 5.d4 Bd7 6.O-O Ng6 7.Ng5 h6 8.Nxf7 Kxf7 9.Bc4+ Ke7?? (9...Ke8 is better, so that 10.Qh5 can be met with ...Qf6) 10.Qh5 Qe8?? (better was to give up the knight) 11.Qg5+ hxg5 12.Bxg5# 1-0","ideas":[{"type":"line","moves":["d7d6","d2d4","c8d7","e1g1","e7g6","f3g5","h7h6","g5f7","e8f7","b5c4","f7e7","d1h5","d8e8","h5g5","h6g5","c1g5"],"verdict":"whiteWinning"}]}],"games":[41474,128886,237965,268881,187911]},{"uci":"d2d4","games":[51203,137148,209771,178472,87093]}]},
