"r1bqk2r/2p1bppp/p1np1n2/1p2p3/4P3/1B3N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQR1K1 w kq -":{"variation":"closed-ruy-lopez","moves":[{"uci":"d2d4","book":true,"games":[470,1642,4726,9603,11782]},{"uci":"c2c3","notes":[{"text":"Your c-pawn gives the bishop a retreat square and prepares the d4 push.","original":"Instead, it must be prepared with 8.c3. With White's kingside fully developed there is no reason to delay this useful move any longer.\n\nWith White preparing to strike in the centre with d4, Black's king typically vacates the premises with 8...O-O. The old-fashioned move is 8...Na5, which may transpose, but the loss of control over d4 may allow White to save a tempo by omitting h3.","ideas":[{"type":"plan","side":"white","routes":[{"from":"d2","to":"d4","moves":1}]}]}],"games":[2501,9625,29882,85123,179284]},{"uci":"h2h3","games":[477,2166,9444,34833,72539]}],"notes":[{"text":"Playing d4 at once falls into the Noah's Ark trap: Black wins your bishop.","original":"Whenever White opens with 1.e4, her most reliable plan is then to prepare to play d4. There is a very good reason not to play 8.d4? instantly - click and learn.\n\nWhite counts the number of pieces attacking d4, and the number of pieces defending it, and reckons that 8.d4 is a safe move.\n\nHowever, 8...Nxd4 9.Nxd4 exd4 10.Qxd4?? and Black has 10...c5!. Wherever White's queen moves, Black follows up with 11...c4 and the bishop is trapped. This motif is called the Noah's Ark trap, because that's how old it is.\n\n10.c3, treating the attack as a gambit, is occasionally played but White has insufficient compensation.\n\nLeon Rosen tried this line against Mikhail Chigorin in Paris in 1900. Chigorin did not play the best move, 8...Nxd4. He continued 8...Bg5, pinning the knight and allowing White to secure the pawn with 9.c3.","ideas":[{"type":"trap","moves":["d2d4","c6d4","f3d4","e5d4","d1d4","c7c5","d4d3","c5c4"],"verdict":"blackClearly"}]}]},
"r1bqk2r/2p1bppp/p1np1n2/1p2p3/4P3/1BP2N2/PP1P1PPP/RNBQR1K1 b kq -":{"variation":"closed-ruy-lopez","moves":[{"uci":"e8g8","notes":[{"text":"Pushing d4 at once walks into ...Bg4, so you usually play h3 first.","original":"White's most consistent move is 9.d4 freeing the queenside minor pieces and contesting the centre, however, it walks into 9...Bg4 after which the conventional wisdom is that Black has nothing to worry about. For this reason the d4 push is almost always prepared with 9.h3. A few masters have recently attempted to prove that 9.d4 is possible.","ideas":[{"type":"plan","side":"white","routes":[{"from":"h2","to":"h3","moves":1},{"from":"d2","to":"d4","moves":1}]}]}],"games":[2506,9122,24766,61309,122173]},{"uci":"c6a5","games":[166,991,4517,17581,41244]},{"uci":"c8g4","notes":[{"text":"Black's bishop discourages d4; play d3 and bring your queen's knight over to chase it away.","original":"8...Bg4 is seen from time to time, discouraging an instant d4 by White. White will probably play d3 as a temporary measure, aiming to bring the queens knight to the kingside to evict the bishop in order to achieve d4.","ideas":[{"type":"plan","side":"white","routes":[{"from":"d2","to":"d3","moves":1},{"from":"b1","to":"d2","moves":1}]}]}],"games":[323,1619,6465,20741,36229]}]},
