"r1bqkbnr/2pp1ppp/p1n5/1p2p3/B3P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq -":{"variation":"caro-variation","moves":[{"uci":"a4b3","book":true,"notes":[{"text":"Bb3 is the only move: your bishop is saved and now eyes Black's weak f7 pawn.","original":"5.Bb3 is the only move. White preserves their bishop and gets the free tempo to move it onto the dangerous a2-g8 long diagonal, eying Black's vulnerable f7 pawn.\n\nWhite saves their bishop and now pressures Black's vulnerable f7 pawn.\n\n5...g6 has been tried, most successfully by Shakhriyar Mamedyarov with Black in a blitz match against Vladimir Kramnik.","ideas":[{"type":"purpose","from":"b3","relation":"attacks","targets":["f7"]}]}],"games":[556127,1022311,1324800,1275814,950321]},{"uci":"d2d3","games":[55,42,55,48,58]}]},
"r1bqkbnr/2pp1ppp/p1n5/1p2p3/4P3/1B3N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq -":{"variation":"caro-variation","moves":[{"uci":"c8b7","notes":[{"text":"Black's bishop comes out on the long diagonal, the setup Caro preferred.","original":"5...Bb7, taking advantage of having moved the b-pawn to increase the pressure on e5, was the continuation preferred by Horatio Caro and common in the late 19th/early 20th centuries. Developing the kingside bishop instead in the manner of the Classical defence, 5...Bc5 aka the Graz variation, was also common."}],"games":[46352,88537,108990,88895,53436]},{"uci":"c6a5","notes":[{"text":"Black's knight hunts your bishop; it can't escape, so castle and develop instead.","original":"5...Na5 is the Norwegian variation: Black wants to hunt down White's bishop and get the bishop pair. This is the main continuation today in tournament games.\n\nBlack hunts down White's bishop, arguing that the advantage of the bishop pair is worth giving White a tempo to develop.\n\nWhite can't preserve their bishop. They're not in time to play 6.c3? to give it an escape square, and if 6.Bd5? c6 and the bishop again has nowhere to go. So, White instead can thank Black for the extra tempo, focus on development, and retake when the time comes.\n\nThough Black seems to have left their e-pawn hanging, as is often the case in the Ruy Lopez it is not so simple. 6.Nxe5?! Nxb3 7.axb3 Qg5! 8.Nf3 Qxg2 (recovers the pawn) 9.Rg1 Qh3 10.Rg3 Qe6 .\n\nSince White's bishop doesn't have anywhere to go, Black doesn't need to take it immediately. They can wait until White gives the bishop an escape square (e.g. by playing c3) or until the knight is forced to move. In the main line, that's after 6.O-O d6 7.d4 exd4 8.Nxd4 Bb7 9.Bd2 (threatening Bxa5) Nxb3.\n\n5...Na5 is also known as the counterthrust variation or the Taimanov variation. Mark Taimanov played the line several times in the 1955 USSR Championship, including against Boris Spassky.\n\nIt received the Norwegian soubriquet for Svein Johannessen (1937―2007), a Norwegian IM who adopted the line.","ideas":[{"type":"purpose","from":"a5","relation":"attacks","targets":["b3"]},{"type":"plan","side":"white","routes":[{"from":"e1","to":"g1","moves":1}]}]}],"games":[29001,60930,82602,72714,45681]},{"uci":"g8f6","notes":[{"text":"Black hopes to reach a main-line Spanish, but this move order lets you strike with d4.","original":"5...Nf6!?, aiming to transpose back into a main line Spanish (e.g. 5...Nf6 6.O-O Be7 7.Re1 transposes to a main line Closed Spanish), is possible. This is the usual idea when the 4...b5 is played at the amateur level. However, the move order with 4...b5 and 5...Nf6 is inaccurate because it affords White other options. The most critical move is 6.d4. 6.Ng5?! is sharp but unsound. Black needed to have developed their kingside more before allowing White's pressure on f7.","ideas":[{"type":"plan","side":"white","routes":[{"from":"d2","to":"d4","moves":1}]}]}],"games":[212787,391648,491214,447271,318383]},{"uci":"f8c5","games":[67429,137599,208837,251293,219016]},{"uci":"g7g6","games":[2606,3235,3097,2255,2208]}]},
