"r1bqkbnr/2pp1ppp/p7/np2p3/4P3/1B3N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq -":{"variation":"caro-variation-norwegian-variation","moves":[{"uci":"e1g1","book":true,"notes":[{"text":"Castling develops and threatens Nxe5, since g2 is guarded and Black can't win material back.","original":"Therefore 6.O-O is both an important developing move and threatens Nxe5, because now the g2 pawn is guarded and Black won't be able to recover the material. Therefore Black continues by defending the pawn, before or after taking the bishop, 6.d6 or 6.Nxb3 axb3 7.d6.","ideas":[{"type":"threat","side":"white","move":"f3e5","answer":"d7d6"}]}],"games":[4872,11102,16210,15291,10245]},{"uci":"b3f7","notes":[{"text":"The Nightingale gambit sacrifices the bishop to wreck Black's king; after Kxf7 and Nxe5 you stand much better.","original":"6.Bxf7+, the Nightingale gambit, is a desperado sacrifice. It turns out there was one square the bishop could go to. Rather than trade the bishop for the knight, White will get two pawns and rob Black of castling rights for it instead. The line goes 6...Kxf7 7.Nxe5+ Ke7 8.d4 . 7...Ke8? is a horrific gaffe, because of the weakened kingside: 8.Qh5+ g6 9.Nxg6 hxg6 10.Qxh8 .\n\nAs we can see here, White played the unexpected move 6.Bxf7+!, sacrificing the Bishop to abuse the position of Black after 6...Kxf7 7.Nxe5 7...Kf6 8.Qh5 8...c6 9.Qf7+! & White has a huge advantage. This is known as the Nightingale Gambit. This is by far the most aggressive & sharp move after 5...Na5.","ideas":[{"type":"line","moves":["e8f7","f3e5","f7f6","d1h5","c7c6","h5f7"],"verdict":"whiteClearly"}]}],"games":[1308,3998,7304,8288,5949]},{"uci":"b3d5","games":[6616,10911,10186,4770,1377]},{"uci":"f3e5","games":[5720,11477,16144,16679,12911]}]},
