"r1bqkbnr/1ppp2pp/p1n5/4pp2/B3P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq -":{"variation":"deferred-schliemann","moves":[{"uci":"d2d4","book":true,"notes":[{"text":"Black's best answer is to take on d4, and then you push your e-pawn forward.","original":"5.d4! and Black's retort against d4 in the regular Schliemann, ...fxe4 Nxe5 Nxe5 dxe5 c6, no longer comes with tempo on the bishop. Therefore, Black's queen is unable to develop to the fifth rank with tempo, so 5...fxe4? 6.Nxe5 Nxe5 7.dxe5 c3? White has 8.Nc3. The best continuation is 5...exd4 6.e5 b5 7.Bb3 and Black may play either 7...Na4 or 7...Bb7."}],"games":[67,151,385,931,2513]},{"uci":"b1c3","notes":[{"text":"Nc3 plays out differently here: Black's b5 and b4 kick your knight, and you need d4 later.","original":"5.Nc3, the usual move against the regular Schliemann, also plays out differently here. The inclusion of ...a6 means Black can reply 5...b5 6.Bb3 b4 (kicking the knight) 7.Nd5 fxe4 (attacking the other knight), where White has to find 8.d4!, a shocking knight sacrifice, to stay ahead.","ideas":[{"type":"line","moves":["b7b5","a4b3","b5b4","c3d5","f5e4","d2d4"],"verdict":"whiteSlightly"}]}],"games":[239,511,899,1498,3004]}]},
"r1bqkb1r/1ppp1ppp/p1n5/1B2p3/3Pn3/5N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQ1RK1 w kq -":{"variation":"berlin-defence","moves":[{"uci":"b5c6","book":true,"notes":[{"text":"You need not transpose to the Open Spanish: after the exchange, Qe2 is your better follow-up, pressuring the e-file.","original":"But White doesn't have to transpose into an Open Spanish if they do not want to: 6.Bxc6 dxc6 enters something like the Exchange variation, though there really is a threat of Nxe5 this time. Rather than recover the pawn straight away, White's better line is 7.Qe2, pressuring the e-file while unpinning the d-pawn to prevent a queen trade."}],"games":[1316,2458,3133,2501,1954]},{"uci":"b5a4","notes":[{"text":"Retreating the bishop transposes to the Open Spanish and avoids sidelines like the Wormald attack.","original":"6.Ba4 is a transposition to the Open Spanish. Reaching the Open Spanish via the Berlin defence is a move order trick that avoids sidelines like the Wormald attack."}],"games":[1152,2037,2173,1531,917]}]},
