"r1bqk2r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/1Bb1p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQ1RK1 w kq -":{"variation":"berlin-defence","moves":[{"uci":"f3e5","book":true,"notes":[{"text":"Taking e5 works tactically: if Black recaptures, d4 forks the bishop and knight, though Black can still recover the pawn.","original":"White can take the pawn, 5.Nxe5. Although the e5 pawn appears to be defended by Black's knight, the position of the bishop on c5 means that it is potentially forkable. Therefore, 5...Nxe5 is met with 6.d4, forking the bishop and knight. Nevertheless, Black can safely navigate this and eventually recover the pawn, though Black needs to be aware of the danger of Re1, exploiting the open e-file while their king is still in the centre (e.g. 6...Nxe4? 7.dxc5 Nxc5 8.Re1!)."}],"games":[9970,22268,38111,53261,55847]},{"uci":"c2c3","notes":[{"text":"You prepare d4 and offer the e4 pawn, which your queen can easily win back from e2.","original":"5.c3 preparing d4, is the most common continuation and more straight-forward than the complicated 5.Nxe5 lines. 5.c3 is called the Zukertort gambit: White is actually offering Black their e-pawn, 5...Nxe4, but they can recover it easily enough after 6.Qe2.\n\nWhite leaves their e4 pawn undefended.\n\nWith White having now played c3, 5...a6? is a mistake. White's pawn on c3 now prevents Black's standard exchange Spanish rejoinder Qd4. 5...a6? 6.Bxc6 dxc6 7.Nxe4 and 7...Qd4 is not possible due to the pawn on c3.","ideas":[{"type":"plan","side":"white","routes":[{"from":"d2","to":"d4","moves":1}]}]},{"text":"With your pawn on c3, Black's usual queen trick on d4 is gone, so you win e5.","original":"5.c3 preparing d4, is the most common continuation and more straight-forward than the complicated 5.Nxe5 lines. 5.c3 is called the Zukertort gambit: White is actually offering Black their e-pawn, 5...Nxe4, but they can recover it easily enough after 6.Qe2.\n\nWhite leaves their e4 pawn undefended.\n\nWith White having now played c3, 5...a6? is a mistake. White's pawn on c3 now prevents Black's standard exchange Spanish rejoinder Qd4. 5...a6? 6.Bxc6 dxc6 7.Nxe4 and 7...Qd4 is not possible due to the pawn on c3.","ideas":[{"type":"trap","moves":["a7a6","b5c6","d7c6","f3e5"],"verdict":"whiteSlightly"}]}],"games":[53859,126282,215334,268673,260217]}]},
"r1bqkb1r/pppp1ppp/2n5/1B2p3/4P1n1/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQ1RK1 w kq -":{"variation":"berlin-defence","moves":[{"uci":"h2h3","book":true,"notes":[{"text":"Black's h5 sets a trap: taking the knight opens the h-file against your king, so let it be.","original":"After 5.h3 h5 sets a trap: the bait is the g4 knight, the h3 pawn is the fish, and the h5 pawn is the hook. Black appears to blunder his knight, but 6.hxg4?? hxg4 opens the h-file onto White's castled king, and attacks the f3 knight. Trying to save the knight leads to forced mate, 7.Nh2?? Qh4!. White's best recourse is to allow the knight to be captured.","ideas":[{"type":"line","moves":["h7h5","h3g4","h5g4","f3h2","d8h4"],"verdict":"blackWinning"}]}]}]},
