"r1bqk1nr/pppp2pp/2n5/1Bb1pp2/4P3/2P2N2/PP1P1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq -":{"variation":"classical-defence-central-variation-cordel-gambit","moves":[{"uci":"e4f5","book":true,"notes":[{"text":"Taking the gambit lets Black play e4 against your knight; Nd4 lets Black regain the pawn with more space.","original":"5.exf5, accepting the gambit, allows 5...e4 to hit White's knight. Trying to save it with 6.Nd4 leads to Black recovering the pawn and taking over more space: 6...Nxd4 7.cxd4 Bxd4 8.Be2. Alternatively, White can trade it with 6.d4, but has to accept losing their centre and a poorer pawn structure (6...exf3 7.dxc5 Qe7+ 8.Be3 fxg2 9.Rg1 Nf6, see diagram).","ideas":[{"type":"line","moves":["e5e4","f3d4","c6d4","c3d4","c5d4","b5e2"],"verdict":"blackSlightly"}]}],"games":[151,318,655,1197,1981]},{"uci":"d2d4","notes":[{"text":"Turning down the gambit is strongest; trades leave you an open position and the bishop pair.","original":"5.d4, declining the gambit, is the stronger riposte. If 5...fxe4 6.dxc5 exf3 7.Qxf3, and White is happy with the open position and the bishop pair (see diagram). If 5...exd4? White must be sure to play 6.e5, moving their e-pawn out of danger, before they recapture on d4.","ideas":[{"type":"purpose","from":"d4","relation":"attacks","targets":["c5"]},{"type":"line","moves":["f5e4","d4c5","e4f3","d1f3"],"verdict":"whiteSlightly"}]}],"games":[235,506,1041,1725,3317]}]},
"r1bqk1nr/pppp2pp/2n5/1Bb1pp2/3PP3/2P2N2/PP3PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq -":{"variation":"classical-defence-central-variation-cordel-gambit","moves":[{"uci":"f5e4"}]},
"r1bqkbnr/pppp2pp/2n5/1B2pp2/4P3/2N2N2/PPPP1PPP/R1BQK2R b KQkq -":{"variation":"schliemann-defence","moves":[{"uci":"f5e4","notes":[{"text":"Taking on e4 is Black's only sensible move, since the whole point of ...f5 was to hit your pawn.","original":"4...fxe4 is the only move - otherwise why bother attacking White's e-pawn?"}]}]},
"r1bqkbnr/pppp2pp/2n5/1B2p3/4p3/2N2N2/PPPP1PPP/R1BQK2R w KQkq -":{"variation":"schliemann-defence","moves":[{"uci":"c3e4","notes":[{"text":"Recapturing on e4 wins your pawn back; Black can now build a pawn centre with d5 or play Nf6.","original":"5.Nxe4 is necessary to recover the pawn.\n\nBlack may now accept the invitation to build a classical pawn centre with 5...d5. If White were forced to retreat the e4-knight, this would be enough to put 4.Nc3 out of business. Alas, Black players at GM level have been scoring very badly out of the well-analysed complications that follow White's critical reply 6.Nxe5!\n\nSo Schliemann Defence champion Teimour Radjabov prefers the more sedate 5...Nf6, with the character of the resulting positions depending on whether White now exchanges on f6 voluntarily or has to be forced into it with 6.Qe2 d5."}],"games":[5260,10047,15684,23577,38768]},{"uci":"d1e2","games":[0,1,5,3,8]}]},
"r1bqkbnr/pppp2pp/2n5/1B2p3/4N3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/R1BQK2R b KQkq -":{"variation":"schliemann-defence","moves":[{"uci":"d7d5","notes":[{"text":"Black's pawn centre invites the strike Nxe5, which dynamites it and leaves you in good shape.","original":"This is a great time to dynamite Black's proud pawn centre with 6.Nxe5! White comes out of the resulting tactics in good shape.\n\n6.Ng3 isn't exactly a bad move, and Black still has to do something about the threat to the e-pawn, but who wants to let Black play ...e5 and ...d5 in the first 5 moves and get away with it?","ideas":[{"type":"line","moves":["f3e5","d5e4","e5c6"],"verdict":"whiteSlightly"}]}],"games":[2394,4537,6542,10336,17426]},{"uci":"g8f6","notes":[{"text":"Black's knight challenges your unprotected knight on e4; you can trade on f6 or play Qe2 first.","original":"Black has challenged White's unprotected e4-knight with another knight. This is a problem and an opportunity for White. In the 5...d5 lines this knight doesn't have the opportunity of gaining a tempo by swapping itself for a Black piece of equal value. Here, it does! So an obvious move here would be\n\n6.Nxf6+, the Exchange Variation. Why might White have second thoughts about 6.Nxf6+? After all, if White defends the knight then it's 100% going to get kicked away from e4 on the very next move with ...d5. The answer may lie in Black's options for recapturing on f6. For reasons of both development and pawn structure it looks as though Black would prefer to use the queen and not the g-pawn, but if White now indirectly threatens Black's e-pawn with\n\n6.Qe2, then 6...d5 7.Nxf6+ Qxf6? doesn't work because 8.Qxe5+ would just pick up a pawn, so Black would have to go 7...gxf6 instead. The immediate 6.Nxf6+ could be met effectively by 6...Qxf6.\n\nThis is excellent logic, but chess opening theory doesn't always reward logic. The more honest answer to whether 6.Qe2 is better than 6.Nxf6+ is \"try them and see!\" 6.Nxf6+, for one reason or another, outscores 6.Qe2 both in White wins and Black defeats.","ideas":[{"type":"purpose","from":"f6","relation":"attacks","targets":["e4"]}]}],"games":[1636,3467,6532,10751,19380]}]},
